    github.com/tetratelabs/wazero v1.5.0              Apache-2.0
    golang.org/x/exp v0.0.0-20231006140011-7918f672742d   BSD-3-Clause
    golang.org/x/text v0.13.0                         BSD-3-Clause

  github.com/danlock/pkg: the licence of the version we use cannot be
  determined. The module at v0.0.17-a9828f2 contains no LICENSE, COPYING or
  equivalent file, in the module cache or in the repository at that tag. The
  upstream repository's default branch does carry an Apache-2.0 LICENSE, added
  after that tag, and GitHub reports the repository as Apache-2.0 today — but
  that is a statement about a later commit, not about the bytes ovrin builds
  against. Absent a licence in the released version, the grant is unclear, and
  saying so is more useful than inferring one. This is tracked as a gap to
  close with upstream, not as a settled attribution.

  gogosseract embeds a WebAssembly build of Tesseract in
  internal/wasm/tesseract-core.wasm and ships no separate attribution for what
  went into it. Inspecting the binary shows it links Tesseract, Leptonica,
  libpng and zlib. Tesseract is Apache-2.0 and the tesseract-wasm project the
  build derives from is BSD-2-Clause, both verified upstream and both within
  the policy. Leptonica is distributed under the Leptonica licence, a
  BSD-2-Clause variant that automated classifiers decline to map to an SPDX
  identifier; it is BSD-like in substance and permits redistribution, but it is
  named here as its own licence rather than folded into BSD-2-Clause.
  Attribution for the libraries inside a compiled artefact is properly the
  upstream module's job; it is recorded here because ovrin ships the artefact.


